  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 237 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCompared to yesterday's model suite, the extended forecast period is \nbeginning to trend slightly more active. The primary synoptic \nfeature remains a shortwave trough forecast to dig southeastward \ntoward Iowa on Wednesday. While previous model runs depicted this \nsystem with a much drier air mass, recent guidance indicates greater \nmoisture advection into the region ahead of a developing coastal \nsystem along the Atlantic Seaboard. This increase in available \nmoisture has prompted the introduction of isolated to slight chance \nPoP across the forecast area on Wednesday, primarily confined to the \nfar western counties of the CWA.\n\nConcurrently, a southern-stream trough and an extratropical wave are \nprojected to phase as they develop across the Deep South and off the \nFlorida coast, respectively. These features are forecast to deepen \nand track northward as a Nor'easter from Wednesday through the \nThanksgiving holiday. Although the strongest synoptic-scale forcing \nwill remain anchored along the East Coast, the far western fringe of \nthe system's comma head may brush eastern Iowa, introducing the \nfirst potential for light snow to the region on Black Friday. Given \nthe extended lead time, forecast confidence remains low; however, a \nslight chance for flurries is warranted across the far eastern \ncounties of the DMX CWA on Friday before surface high pressure \nbuilds back into the region behind the departing system. Outside of \nthe large-scale synoptic forcing, daytime highs will start the \nperiod in the 30s before gradually moderating into the mid-40s, with \novernight lows following a similar subtle warming trend.\n\nOverall, the extended period trends slightly more active with low-\nend precipitation chances mid-to-late week, highlighted by a slight \nchance for light snow in far eastern Iowa on Black Friday alongside \na gradual warming trend into the mid-40s.",
